- Measles surveillance strengthened in Paraná after autochthonous cases were confirmed in São Paulo. Paraná's State Health Secretariat (Sesa) reports no confirmed measles since June 2020 (an imported case in Curitiba). In 2026, 73 suspected cases in Paraná: 71 discarded, 2 under investigation; 2025 had 259 notifications all discarded, and 2024 had 125 suspicions with no confirmations. The emergence of local transmission in São Paulo raises concern due to interstate movement and cross-border exposure with Paraguay; Sesa is coordinating with Paraguay to reinforce vaccination and sanitary barriers at border areas. Vaccination efforts include extramural measles vaccination at major airports (Curitiba, Maringá, Foz do Iguaçu, Londrina, Cascavel) with Anvisa; border towns Foz do Iguaçu and Guaíra;
- Measles risk prompts reinforced vigilance in Paraná after autochthonous cases were confirmed in São Paulo. Paraná, which has not recorded a confirmed case since June 2020, logged 73 suspected measles in 2026, with 71 discarded and two under investigation; all 2025 suspicions (259) and 2024 suspicions (125) were discarded. Proximity to São Paulo and cross-border movement heighten concern, prompting intensified vaccination and dialogue with Paraguay to bolster border protection. Since early 2026, Paraná has conducted extramuros measles vaccination at Curitiba, Maringá, Foz do Iguaçu, Londrina, and Cascavel airports, in partnership with ANVISA. The strategy extends to border municipalities, notably Foz do Iguaçu and Guaíra, with joint vaccination campaigns and border health measures. Training
